# RAGSTRIKE -- one .gitignore for all three applications.
#
# The three projects (RAGStrike, SecureRAG, VulnerableRAG) share the same stack, so they generate
# the same kinds of artifact in the same places. Patterns without a leading slash match at any
# depth, which is what lets one file at the root cover all of them.
#
# THE RULE THIS FOLLOWS
#   Commit what a human wrote. Ignore what a machine can regenerate.
#
#   Everything below is regenerable: virtualenvs from `pip install -e`, corpora from
#   `scripts/seed_corpus.py`, reports from a scan. None of it belongs in a repository, and the
#   virtualenvs alone are 2.6 GB -- against roughly 6 MB of actual source.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Virtualenvs -- 2.6 GB across the three projects. Rebuilt by the install step in GUIDE/01.
#
# They are also NOT PORTABLE: a venv records absolute paths and a platform. This project was
# developed on Windows and moved to Linux, and the copied `.venv` folders contained `Scripts/` and
# `Lib/` instead of `bin/` and `lib/` -- they could not run and had to be deleted and rebuilt.
# Committing one guarantees the next person inherits that same broken state.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Archived per-project git histories.
#
# The three applications each used to be their own repository, nested inside this one. Git tracked
# them as gitlinks (mode 160000) with no `.gitmodules`, so GitHub rendered three empty grey folders
# and NONE of the source was ever actually pushed -- roughly a thousand files, invisible.
#
# Flattening them into this repository required their `.git` directories to stop existing where git
# would find them. They were RENAMED rather than deleted, so the old commit history is still on
# disk and recoverable; neither had a remote, so nothing existed online to lose. Recover one with:
#
#     mv RAGStrike/.git-archive-RAGStrike RAGStrike/.git
#
# Ignored because that history belongs to a layout this repository no longer uses.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git-archive-*/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python build and cache artifacts
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test, lint, and type-checker caches
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.hypothesis/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lab runtime state -- the ingested corpora and their databases.
#
# REGENERATE, DO NOT COMMIT. `scripts/seed_corpus.py` rebuilds all of it from the PDFs in
# `corpus/`, which ARE committed. The embeddings are a derived index of documents already in the
# repository, and a ChromaDB directory carries an exclusive lock plus absolute paths, so a
# committed one is a merge conflict waiting to happen rather than a convenience.
#
# There is a correctness reason too: the differential comparison is only meaningful while both labs
# hold the SAME documents. A vector store committed from one machine and half-updated on another is
# exactly the state that produces a difference which looks like a security control and is really a
# difference in what got loaded.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
vectorstore/chroma/
data/
*.db
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Documents uploaded through the UI at runtime. The seed corpus lives in `corpus/` and is tracked;
# anything that arrives here came from a demo and is not part of the project.
uploads/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated output -- logs and scan reports
#
# `logs/` reaches 6 MB after a few scans, and every line is reproducible by running the scan again.
#
# `reports/` is the judgement call. Each directory is one scan's HTML/JSON/MD/PDF output, named by
# scan id. They are evidence of a run rather than part of the tool, they are regenerated on every
# scan, and committing them means the repository grows with use. If a specific report is wanted as
# a demo artifact, add it deliberately with `git add -f`.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
logs/
*.log
reports/*/
!reports/README.md

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor, OS, and secrets
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Nothing in this project needs a secret -- the canaries are synthetic by design and are committed
# on purpose. This is here so that stays true by accident as well as by intent.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets.yaml

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored, so nobody adds them later
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   corpus/benign/     -- the reference documents; the labs are meaningless without them
#   corpus/poisoned/   -- the attack documents, with their payloads documented in seed_corpus.py
#   sample-corpus/     -- the demo documents, deliberately not pre-ingested
#   configs/           -- thresholds and profiles are the experiment; they must be reviewable
#   .streamlit/        -- pins the loopback bind address and the base theme
